MMS HTTP download plan
======================

STATUS: RESOLVED (2026-07-11)
------------------------------
End-to-end MMS content download now works on real hardware: 6/6 test notifications
(text-only and image/GIF-containing) fetched, decoded, and stored successfully via
the API. Root causes and fixes, in the order they were found:

1. AT+QHTTPCFG="proxy" is not a supported key on this firmware (confirmed against
   the local HTTP(S) application guide -- AT+QHTTPCFG=? only lists contextid,
   requestheader, responseheader, sslctxid, contenttype, rspout/auto, closed/ind,
   windowsize, closewaittime, custom_header). This is why it always failed with
   +CME ERROR: 730 (Invalid parameter).
2. Fix: use AT+QHTTPCFG="requestheader",1 and point AT+QHTTPURL at the carrier
   PROXY address (not the content host) so the modem's TCP socket connects to
   something actually reachable over the MMS APN. The custom request block sent
   after AT+QHTTPGET carries the real absolute content URL as the request line
   (mirrors how a standard HTTP forward proxy request works). Falls back to the
   old direct-fetch path (no proxy) if requestheader mode fails.
3. In requestheader mode, AT+QHTTPGET takes <rsptime>,<data_length>[,<input_time>]
   -- the original code only sent <rsptime>, which also produced CME ERROR 730.
4. Self-deadlock: the requestheader write sequence held `write_half`'s lock across
   the async QHTTPGET completion wait and the subsequent AT+QHTTPREADFILE call,
   which needs the same lock via the command processor -- fetches larger than the
   first item hung forever. Fixed by dropping the guard right after the writes.
4. AT+QFDWL raw-response framing treated the interim `CONNECT\r\n` prompt as a
   valid completion terminator, so small buffers containing just `CONNECT\r\n`
   could be dispatched before the real payload/trailer arrived. Fixed with a
   raw-mode-specific terminator set (RAW_TERMINATORS) that excludes CONNECT.
5. Root cause for larger MMS (100KB+, e.g. embedded images): AT+QHTTPREADFILE
   returns `OK` as soon as the command is accepted, not once the file is actually
   written -- issuing AT+QFDWL immediately after raced the file write and read a
   0-byte file. Fixed by waiting for the async `+QHTTPREADFILE: <err>` completion
   URC (new pending_http_readfile waiter, mirroring pending_http_get) before
   calling download_file().
6. Also removed the doomed AT+QHTTPCFG="proxy" attempt from the direct-fetch
   fallback (dead code that only produced log noise/730 errors on this firmware).

Verified via live retry against real modem (COM8, SIM 898600801926D1412749):
c812f386 (SMIL+text), 91ff4bd1 (SMIL+jpeg 110798B), 02bad003 (SMIL+text),
10f60912 (SMIL+text), cabdca41 (SMIL+gif 118828B+text), cbfa0765 (SMIL+text) --
all reached status=fetched with correctly decoded parts.

Goal
----
Make MMS inbox content download work reliably on Quectel EC20/EC25 class modems for carrier MMS APNs that require a proxy.

Current confirmed findings
--------------------------
1. MMS notification detection already works.
2. Existing fetch path uses AT+QHTTPURL / AT+QHTTPGET / AT+QHTTPREADFILE.
3. Real modem logs showed:
   - +QHTTPGET: 702
   - direct HTTP fetch timed out
4. The original QHTTPGET URC parser was too strict and was fixed to accept the single-value error form.
5. Attempting:
   AT+QHTTPCFG="proxy","10.0.0.172:80"
   was rejected by modem with:
   +CME ERROR: 730
6. Quectel FAE forum guidance indicates:
   - QMMSCFG proxy does not automatically apply to QHTTP
   - direct "set HTTP proxy" is not effective
   - they recommend QHTTPCFG="requestheader",1 and adding proxy information in custom HTTP headers
7. The forum thread does NOT show a complete working GET example, so the exact requestheader flow still needs confirmation from local PDF manuals.

Likely root cause
-----------------
The MMS APN only allows MMS retrieval through the carrier proxy (for this SIM: 10.0.0.172:80).
AT+QHTTPGET appears to connect directly to the content_location host unless special handling is enabled.
Because the direct path is blocked or unroutable on the MMS APN, the modem returns timeout 702.

