Implementation paths
--------------------
Option A - Keep QHTTP and use requestheader mode
Preferred only if manuals confirm a complete GET flow.

Needed behavior:
1. Enable custom header mode:
   AT+QHTTPCFG="requestheader",1
2. Build a request that causes the carrier proxy to fetch the MMS URL.
3. Possible required request shape to test:
   GET http://120.198.247.46:80/DFUMoT3 HTTP/1.1
   Host: 120.198.247.46
   X-Online-Host: 120.198.247.46
   Proxy-Connection: Keep-Alive

Open questions:
- How Quectel expects custom GET headers to be supplied.
- Whether QHTTPURL payload becomes the full request line in requestheader mode, or only the URL.
- Whether Host / absolute-URI request line alone is enough.

Option B - Replace QHTTP with raw TCP socket proxy fetch
Recommended fallback if requestheader mode is incomplete or unreliable.

High-level flow:
1. Open TCP socket to MMS proxy host:port using QIOPEN on context 1.
2. Send raw HTTP request through the proxy:
   GET http://<content-host>:<port><path> HTTP/1.1
   Host: <content-host>
   Connection: close

3. Read raw HTTP response using QIRD until full headers/body received.
4. Parse status line, headers, Content-Length, and body.
5. Store body in DB exactly like current fetch_mms_content output.

Why Option B may be safer
-------------------------
It does not depend on undocumented or poorly documented QHTTP requestheader behavior.
It matches how a normal HTTP forward proxy really works.
It gives full control over the request line and headers.

